  {"id":128824,"slug":"lucilia-sericata","sci":"Lucilia sericata","common":"Common European Greenbottle Fly","family":"Calliphoridae","obs":1984,"regions":["north-cascades","central-cascades","gorge","olympics"],"months":[4,5,6,7,8,9,10],"photos":[{"id":17295643,"ext":"jpg","by":"an iNaturalist observer","lic":"CC0"},{"id":685230,"ext":"jpg","by":"Marcello Consolo","lic":"CC BY-NC-SA"},{"id":30304685,"ext":"jpg","by":"bob15noble","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":74557161,"ext":"jpg","by":"Nadja Baumgartner","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Lucilia sericata","summary":"The common green bottle fly is a blow fly found in most areas of the world, and the most well-known of the numerous green bottle fly species. It is 10–14 mm long, slightly larger than a house fly, and has brilliant, metallic, blue-green or golden coloration with black markings. It has short, sparse black bristles and three cross-grooves on the thorax."},
  {"id":194786,"slug":"merodon-equestris","sci":"Merodon equestris","common":"Narcissus Bulb Fly","family":"Syrphidae","obs":1975,"regions":["central-cascades","olympics","oregon-cascades"],"months":[5,6],"photos":[{"id":788109,"ext":"jpg","by":"S. Rae","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":19808255,"ext":"jpg","by":"Bill Rodstrom","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":788112,"ext":"jpg","by":"JerryL2008","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":11356095,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Todd Boland","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":1218537,"ext":"","by":"Steve Kerr","lic":"CC BY"}],"wiki":"Merodon equestris","summary":"Merodon equestris is a Holarctic species of hoverfly. Like many other hoverflies it displays a colouration pattern similar to a stinging insect as an evolutionary defense mechanism. Other syrphid bee mimics are Mallota, Arctophila, Criorhina, Pocota and Brachypalpus. Merodon species are distinguished from these by the very strong hind femora, which bear a large triangular projection on"},
