  {"id":258813,"slug":"clogmia-albipunctata","sci":"Clogmia albipunctata","common":"Bathroom Moth Fly","family":"Psychodidae","obs":1037,"regions":["north-cascades","central-cascades","south-cascades","gorge","olympics"],"months":[1,5,6,7,8,9,10,11],"photos":[{"id":385527160,"ext":"jpg","by":"Sakern | 永隔一江水","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":6183053,"ext":"jpg","by":"Katja Schulz","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":1524395,"ext":"jpg","by":"Katja Schulz","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":171275378,"ext":"jpg","by":"Trey Wardlaw","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Clogmia albipunctata","summary":"Drain flies, sink flies, filter flies, or sewer gnats are small true flies with short, hairy bodies and wings giving them a \"furry\" moth-like appearance, hence one of their common names, moth flies. There are more than 4,700 known species worldwide, most of them native to the humid tropics. This makes them one of the most diverse families of their order."},
