  {"id":82177,"slug":"hermetia-illucens","sci":"Hermetia illucens","common":"Black Soldier Fly","family":"Stratiomyidae","obs":881,"regions":["gorge","oregon-cascades"],"months":[6,7,8,9,10],"photos":[{"id":97683465,"ext":"jpg","by":"Katja Schulz","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":1106319,"ext":"jpg","by":"Marcello Consolo","lic":"CC BY-NC-SA"},{"id":2290215,"ext":"jpg","by":"Marcello Consolo","lic":"CC BY-NC-SA"},{"id":1840415,"ext":"jpg","by":"Marcello Consolo","lic":"CC BY-NC-SA"}],"wiki":"Hermetia illucens","summary":"Hermetia illucens, the black soldier fly, is a common and widespread fly of the family Stratiomyidae. The larvae and adults are considered neither pests nor vectors. Instead, black soldier fly larvae play a similar role to that of redworms as essential decomposers in breaking down organic substrates and returning nutrients to the soil."},
