  {"id":491047,"slug":"eristalis-anthophorina","sci":"Eristalis anthophorina","common":"Orange-spotted Drone Fly","family":"Syrphidae","obs":846,"regions":["olympics"],"months":[4,5,6,7,8,9],"photos":[{"id":9169896,"ext":"jpg","by":"Jimmy Dee","lic":"CC BY-SA"},{"id":8015817,"ext":"jpg","by":"Don Sutherland","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":9422338,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Denis Doucet","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":55761667,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Denis Doucet","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Eristalis anthophorina","summary":"Eristalis anthophorina, the orange-spotted drone fly, is a species of syrphid fly with a Holarctic distribution. IT is a common fly in wetlands, including bogs, fens, and woodland pools. In North America, it occurs throughout much of Canada and primarily in the northern parts of the United States. It reaches around 15 mm (0.6 in) in length."},
  {"id":428810,"slug":"lapposyrphus-lapponicus","sci":"Lapposyrphus lapponicus","common":"Common Loopwing Aphideater","family":"Syrphidae","obs":733,"regions":["north-cascades","central-cascades","rainier","south-cascades","gorge","olympics","oregon-cascades"],"months":[3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10],"photos":[{"id":2048622,"ext":"jpg","by":"Denis Doucet","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":43219911,"ext":"jpeg","by":"nicky-by-nature","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":14524947,"ext":"jpg","by":"Denis Doucet","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":73255337,"ext":"jpg","by":"Jeremiah Degenhardt","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":14369821,"ext":"jpg","by":"Denis Doucet","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Lapposyrphus lapponicus","summary":"Lapposyrphus lapponicus (Zetterstedt, 1838), the common loopwing aphideater (in North America) or Lapland syrphid fly (in Europe), is a common species of syrphid fly observed across the Northern Hemisphere. The adults are commonly found on flowers from which they get both energy-giving nectar and protein rich pollen. Larvae feed on aphids."},
