  {"id":311582,"slug":"drosophila-suzukii","sci":"Drosophila suzukii","common":"Spotted-winged Drosophila","family":"Drosophilidae","obs":247,"regions":["olympics"],"months":[1,6,7,8,9,10,11,12],"photos":[{"id":7114784,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Andrea Peterlongo","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":11977486,"ext":"jpeg","by":"carnifex","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":4700909,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Scott Gilmore","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":52432465,"ext":"jpg","by":"Hans Holbrook","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":252516253,"ext":"jpg","by":"solomon v. hendrix","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Drosophila suzukii","summary":"Drosophila suzukii, commonly called the spotted wing drosophila, is a vinegar fly—closely related to Drosophila melanogaster (the common vinegar fly). D. suzukii, originally from southeast Asia, is becoming a major pest species in America and Europe, because it infests fruit early during the ripening stage, in contrast with other Drosophila species that infest only rotting fruit."},
